<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Confederation of the Rhine</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1813 CE · capital Frankfurt am Main</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleon's league of German client states, whose creation in 1806 brought the end of the Holy Roman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 12 July 1806 sixteen German states, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-confederation-act">Rhine Confederation Act</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, leaving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> protection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> abdicated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> on 6 August 1806. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and many smaller states joined later, bringing it to some 39 members.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was its protector and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a> its prince-primate. A federal diet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> was planned but never met. Members were bound to a military alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and had to supply contingents, notably for the 1812 Russian campaign. Within their borders many states introduced Napoleonic reforms, ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and absorbed mediatised lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in October 1813, its members changed sides one by one and the confederation dissolved. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> replaced it in 1815 with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/confederation-of-the-rhine.jpg" alt="Confederation of the Rhine" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confederation_of_the_Rhine_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1813 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Frankfurt am Main</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Napoleon I, Karl Theodor von Dalberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany, Austria, Poland, Liechtenstein</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rheinbund, Rhenish Confederation</dd></dl></aside></div>
